Here's a minimal C++ example using the Gridlock Labs SDK to load a road network from OSM and run 100 simulation steps:

```cpp
#include <gridlock/simulator.h>
#include <gridlock/osm_loader.h>

int main() {
    // Initialize the simulator
    gridlock::Simulator sim;

    // Load OSM file
    gridlock::OSMLoader loader;
    loader.loadFromFile("path/to/your/file.osm");
    sim.setNetwork(loader.getNetwork());

    // Set simulation time step (in seconds)
    sim.setTimeStep(1.0);

    // Run 100 steps
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {
        sim.step();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Make sure to replace `"path/to/your/file.osm"` with the actual path to your OpenStreetMap file. Also, ensure you link against the Gridlock SDK libraries when compiling.

For more advanced usage, such as adding vehicles or sensors, please consult the SDK documentation or contact us at devhelp@gridlocklabs.example for assistance.

Sprocket here! Great question — making a button change color when you click it is like giving it a fun surprise every time you tap it! Let’s walk through a simple way to do this using HTML and JavaScript.

### Step 1: Create the Button in HTML
First, we’ll make a button in your HTML file:

```html
<button id="myButton">Click Me!</button>
```

This gives our button an ID called `myButton`, which we’ll use in JavaScript to find it.

---

### Step 2: Add Some Style in CSS (Optional)
You can style the button to look nice:

```css
#myButton {
  padding: 10px 20px;
  font-size: 16px;
  background-color: lightblue;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

---

### Step 3: Add JavaScript to Change the Color
Now, let’s make the button change color when you click it. Add this to your JavaScript:

```javascript
// Find the button using its ID
const button = document.getElementById("myButton");

// Add a click event listener
button.addEventListener("click", function() {
  // Change the button's background color
  button.style.backgroundColor = "lightgreen";
});
```

Every time you click the button, it will switch from light blue to light green!

---

### Bonus: Make It Toggle Between Colors
Want the button to switch back and forth? Here's a version that toggles between two colors:

```javascript
button.addEventListener("click", function() {
  if (button.style.backgroundColor === "lightgreen") {
    button.style.backgroundColor = "lightblue";
  } else {
    button.style.backgroundColor = "lightgreen";
  }
});
```

---

